A||N||6||mindscape||440T4/4T60 TCC Lockup||||5||
Z||000000||mindscape||07-17-2001||02:04 AM||||Okay, so now that I have a 440T4/4T60 4spd auto transaxle... How does the ECM in the cars these units come from the factory control 3 (drive) and 4 (overdrive) TCC lockup?||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||07-17-2001||11:34 AM||||It doesn't. The ECM only controls the TCC. Depending on the source the trans, it may have 3rd and 4th gear switches to tell the ECM what gear it's in. <P>Only the "E" trans can be gear controlled by the ECM (e.g. the 4T60E).<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000002||mindscape||07-17-2001||05:10 PM||||The question is... how to control lockup of the TCC when swaping a 440T4/4T60 in place of a THM125.  I'm not using a 4T60E.  However, the 440T4 and 4T60 can lockup in Drive (3rd gear) or in OverDrive (4th gear).  The Fiero ECM controls lockup of the THM125 in 3rd Gear and can sense that the transaxle is in 3rd.  The 440T4/4T60 use the same switch ... So will I get lockup only in 3rd or only in 4th?<BR>I'd like to get lockup in 3rd and 4th depending on where I'm driving - city/highway.||||reg||5
Z||000003||theogre||07-17-2001||08:03 PM||||The ECM has no clue what gear the tranny is in. There are additional switches inside the tranny in series. When the tranny is in a suitable lockup condition it closes it's switche(s). When all the needed switches are closed, the TC locks.<P>In other words, the tranny controls the gear switches and the ECM controls the speed switch.<P>That I'm aware, the 4 speed plugs into the ECM with the same connector as the TH125. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||07-17-2001||09:50 PM||||Generally, the 3rd gear switch is wired in series with the TCC solenoid and brake switch so the 3rd gear switch must be closed to allow TCC. Since the 3rd gear switch is also closed in 4th gear, you get TCC in 4th too.<P>Then just ignore the 4th gear switch since it's a signal back to the ECM and it's not needed.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mindscape||07-17-2001||10:20 PM||||I'm looking at page 179 in the Haynes repair manual.  I see the series circuit that has been implicated along with the ECM.  The 440T4/4T60 has four wires coming out of it.  Two are for 3rd gear, and I assume two are for 4th gear.  Further, I assume there is good reason.  I don't have a tech book on the transaxle.<P>&gt;&gt;Then just ignore the 4th gear switch since it's a signal back to the ECM and it's not needed.<P>So the extra set of wires is merely feedback?<P>I'm going to a transmission shop tomorrow.  I won't have the warm fuzzies until I see the nitty gritty details for myself.||||reg||5
Z||000006||theogre||07-17-2001||10:59 PM||||I'd have to see the 4 speed switching. I don't have a copy of it anymore. That makes this allot harder.<P>In the end, you want to wire it to lock in 4th. You don't really care if it locks in 3. Unless you lock it in 3 with the shifter, it should not spend any more time in 3 than it has to to get to 4.<P>The ECM won't close the speed switch untill a fairly constant speed over 35 is reached. By this time the tranny will have settled into 4th gear.||||reg||1
